### Define useEffect signature

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/reference/react/useEffect.md

Synchronizes a component with an external system by providing a setup function and an optional list of reactive dependencies.

```javascript
useEffect(setup, dependencies?)
```

--------------------------------

### Add cleanup function to disconnect on Effect cleanup

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/reference/react/StrictMode.md

Returns a cleanup function from useEffect to disconnect outdated connections and resolve the leak.

```javascript
  useEffect(() => {
    const connection = createConnection(serverUrl, roomId);
    connection.connect();
    return () => connection.disconnect();
  }, [roomId]);
```

--------------------------------

### Compare useEffect execution behavior across dependency configurations

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/learn/synchronizing-with-effects.md

Illustrates how omitting the dependency array, passing an empty array `[]`, and passing dependency values `[a, b]` alter when the effect runs.

```js
useEffect(() => {
  // This runs after every render
});

useEffect(() => {
  // This runs only on mount (when the component appears)
}, []);

useEffect(() => {
  // This runs on mount *and also* if either a or b have changed since the last render
}, [a, b]);
```

--------------------------------

### Connecting to an external system using useEffect

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/reference/react/useEffect.md

Call useEffect at the top level of your component to synchronize with an external system. Pass a setup function that initiates the connection and returns a cleanup function to disconnect, along with all reactive dependencies.

```javascript
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { createConnection } from './chat.js';

function ChatRoom({ roomId }) {
  const [serverUrl, setServerUrl] = useState('https://localhost:1234');

  useEffect(() => {
  	const connection = createConnection(serverUrl, roomId);
    connection.connect();
  	return () => {
      connection.disconnect();
  	};
  }, [serverUrl, roomId]);
  // ...
}
```

--------------------------------

### Connect to external chat service with useEffect and cleanup

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/reference/react/useEffect.md

Calls useEffect at the top level of a component to initiate a connection and returns a cleanup function to disconnect when dependencies change or the component unmounts.

```javascript
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { createConnection } from './chat.js';

function ChatRoom({ roomId }) {
  const [serverUrl, setServerUrl] = useState('https://localhost:1234');

  useEffect(() => {
    const connection = createConnection(serverUrl, roomId);
    connection.connect();
    return () => {
      connection.disconnect();
    };
  }, [serverUrl, roomId]);
  // ...
}
```

steps.text:
I looked up useEffect in Context7 using the official React docs (library ID `/reactjs/react.dev`). The main page is `src/content/reference/react/useEffect.md` in the reactjs/react.dev repo.

**What it does and how to call it**
```js
useEffect(setup, dependencies?)
```
useEffect keeps a component in sync with something outside React, like a server connection. You pass it a setup function and, optionally, a list of the values the effect depends on. Call it at the top level of your component.

**When the effect runs** (from `learn/synchronizing-with-effects.md`)
```js
useEffect(() => { /* runs after every render */ });
useEffect(() => { /* runs only on mount */ }, []);
useEffect(() => { /* runs on mount and whenever a or b changes */ }, [a, b]);
```

**Cleanup**
The setup function can return a cleanup function. React runs it when the dependencies change or the component unmounts. The docs' example:
```js
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { createConnection } from './chat.js';

function ChatRoom({ roomId }) {
  const [serverUrl, setServerUrl] = useState('https://localhost:1234');

  useEffect(() => {
    const connection = createConnection(serverUrl, roomId);
    connection.connect();
    return () => {
      connection.disconnect();
    };
  }, [serverUrl, roomId]);
  // ...
}
```
The StrictMode page (`reference/react/StrictMode.md`) shows the same pattern. It explains that returning `() => connection.disconnect()` closes old connections so they don't leak.

Context7 also offered docs for React v18 (`/reactjs/react.dev/__branch__v18`) if you need that version.

